    "counterexample_dsp": "Create table1 with one row (c3=1). Create table2 with two rows for c3=1: one with c6='2019-02-01' (within range) and one with c6='2019-04-01' (outside range). Q1 returns empty result because table2's c3=1 has c6 values outside Jan-Mar 2019. Q2 returns (1, table1.c4) because after joining, the group for c3=1 has MIN(c6)='2019-02-01' and MAX(c6)='2019-04-01', which fails the HAVING condition (MAX > '2019-03-31'), so it also returns empty. Wait, need to adjust: For Q2 to return something when Q1 doesn't, we need a case where after joining, the group's MIN and MAX fall within range despite individual rows outside it. This requires multiple rows in table2 for the same c3 where some c6 are outside range but MIN and MAX are still within range, which is impossible since MIN and MAX would capture the extremes. Actually, the difference is the opposite: Q1 might return rows that Q2 doesn't. For Q1 to return something when Q2 doesn't, we need a c3 where all c6 in table2 are within range (so Q1 includes it), but after joining with table1, if there are multiple rows in table2 for that c3, the group's MIN and MAX still within range, so Q2 would also include it. To get different results, consider when table2 has no rows for a c3. Q1's subquery returns no c3 values, so Q1 returns empty. Q2 performs a join, so if table1 has a c3 not in table2, it won't appear in the join result, so Q2 also returns empty. The real difference: Q1 filters table2 first based on all rows per c3, then joins. Q2 joins all rows, then filters groups. If a c3 in table2 has at least one row with c6 within range and at least one outside, Q1 excludes it entirely. Q2 might include it if, after joining, the group's MIN and MAX happen to be within range, but since MIN and MAX are extremes, if there's any c6 outside range, MAX or MIN will reflect that, so Q2 would also exclude it. Thus, they are equivalent? Wait, consider NULL values in c6. If c6 can be NULL, MIN and MAX ignore NULLs. So if