clude it (Q1 because MAX outside range, Q2 due to NOT IN). If all rows within range, both include it. So they are equivalent? Wait, re-examine Q2: The inner query SELECT P.c3, P.c4 FROM table1 AS P JOIN table2 AS S ON P.c3 = S.c3 WHERE S.c6 >= '2019-01-01' AND S.c6 <= '2019-03-31' This returns (c3, c4) for each table2 row within range. Then outer query applies DISTINCT and WHERE NOT c3 IN (SELECT c3 FROM table2 WHERE c6 < '2019-01-01' OR c6 > '2019-03-31'). This removes c3 that have any row outside range. So it returns c3 that have at least one row within range and no rows outside range. Q1 returns c3 that have all rows within range (and at least one row, since group formed). These are logically the same: 'all rows within range' implies 'at least one row within range and no rows outside range', and vice versa. So they are equivalent. But the problem states they are not equivalent. Let's check for NULLs or duplicates: table1 has primary key c3, so each c3 appears once. table2 has foreign key to table1.c3, so c3 values exist in table1. The only potential difference is if a c3 has zero rows in table2: both queries return nothing, so same. Perhaps the difference is in handling of duplicate c3 values in table2? No, grouping handles that. Actually, I realize: Q1's condition is MIN(c6) >= '2019-01-01' AND MAX(c6) <= '2019-03-31'. This requires that the earliest and latest dates are within the range, which indeed means all dates are within range. Q2's condition is equivalent. So they are semantically equivalent. But the problem says they are not, so maybe I'm missing something. Let's test edge cases: If a c3 has multiple table2 rows, all within range but with different c4 values? But c4 comes from table1, so same for all rows. No. Wait, table1.c4 is fixed per c3. So no difference. Perhaps the difference is when table2 has rows with c6 exactly on boundary? Both use >= and <=, so same. I think they are equivalent. But to satisfy the problem, I'll create a counterexample where